// oob_dynamic_index — Dynamic index out of bounds (requires bounds-check IR, always on in VUMA 2.0)
// Expected exit code: 134
// Compile: compile_dump <file> <out> <backend> --opt-level=O3
//
// Bounds SAFE-ONLY negative test: allocate an 8-byte buffer
// (`[u8; 8]`), seed indices 0/1, then compute `idx = 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10`
// at runtime and read `b.data[idx]`. With bounds-check IR (always on in
// VUMA 2.0), the injected `UGe(idx, 8)` check evaluates to true
// (10 >= 8) and dispatches to `__oob_trap`, aborting with exit 134
// (SIGABRT, 128 + 6).
//
// IMPORTANT: This test is NOT in the regular `bounds_wave1/` gold
// standard suite because it only passes with bounds-check IR injected
// (always on in VUMA 2.0). Without bounds-check IR (legacy mode), the
// OOB access is undefined behaviour. The expected exit code below
// assumes bounds-check IR is injected.
//
// VUMA Key Concepts:
//   - state_new(Layout) allocation
//   - `[u8; 8]` array field
//   - runtime-computed index `idx = 1 + 2 + 3 + 4` — even though the
//     arithmetic folds to a constant 10 at compile time, the resulting
//     `UGe(10, 8)` check folds to TRUE (out of bounds), so the trap
//     branch is statically reachable and survives DCE. This is the
//     complement of `inbounds_dynamic_index.vuma`, where `UGe(2, 32)`
//     folds to FALSE (in bounds) and the trap branch is DCE'd.
//   - exit 134 == SIGABRT emitted by `__oob_trap`.

layout Buf = { data: [u8; 8] }

transform main() -> i32 {
    let b = state_new(Buf);
    b.data[0] = 10;
    b.data[1] = 20;
    // Read at index that depends on runtime computation
    let idx = 1 + 2 + 3 + 4;
    return b.data[idx];
}
